<h2 id="the-free-ride-ends">The Free Ride Ends: Draft2Digital Fee Changes</h2>
<p>In April 2026, Draft2Digital — one of the most popular ebook distribution platforms for indie authors — announced it will begin charging fees for the first time in its decade-long history. New accounts will pay a one-time $20 activation fee, and existing accounts earning under $100 annually will be charged a $12 yearly maintenance fee starting May 14, 2026. The move signals a major shift in the self-publishing landscape and leaves many authors asking: what are the best free alternatives for publishing EPUB files in 2026?</p>
<p>This guide breaks down exactly what Draft2Digital changed, how it affects your bottom line, and which platforms offer the best EPUB-native publishing experience without the new fees.</p>
<hr />

<h2 id="what-changed">What Draft2Digital Changed in 2026</h2>
<p>Draft2Digital — which also owns Smashwords — announced two new fees via email to its author base in mid-April 2026. Here is the exact breakdown:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Fee Type</th><th>Amount</th><th>Who Pays</th><th>Effective Date</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>New account activation</td><td>$20 one-time</td><td>All new accounts</td><td>Immediate (April 2026)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Annual maintenance</td><td>$12/year</td><td>Accounts earning under $100 in 12-month period</td><td>May 14, 2026</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>According to Draft2Digital's announcement, the activation fee is intended to combat "automated and low-quality account creation" and "content farms" that threaten reader trust in indie titles. The annual maintenance fee applies to accounts whose total book sales earnings fall below $100 over the preceding 12 months. Authors earning $100 or more from book sales are exempt from the yearly charge.</p>
<p>This follows a separate royalty restructuring in January 2026, where Draft2Digital and Smashwords simplified their payout model to 75% royalties for ebooks priced at $2.99 or higher and 40% for those below $2.99. Combined, the fee and royalty changes mean that small-volume authors — those earning under $100 annually — now face a significant cost increase.</p>
<hr />

<h2 id="who-is-affected">Who Is Affected by the New Fees</h2>
<p>Understanding whether the Draft2Digital fees matter to you depends on your publishing profile. Here are the three main scenarios:</p>
<h3>Low-Volume Authors (Earning Under $100/Year)</h3>
<p>You are hit hardest. The $12 annual maintenance fee represents a significant percentage of your earnings. If you earn $50/year in royalties, paying $12 means giving up 24% of your income to platform fees alone. For authors publishing one or two books as a side project or hobby, the fee may make Draft2Digital uneconomical.</p>
<h3>Active Self-Publishers (Earning $100+/Year)</h3>
<p>The annual maintenance fee does not apply to you, but you still pay the 10-25% distribution commission. If you signed up before the fee change, you avoid the $20 activation charge. Your primary concern is whether Draft2Digital's commission rate remains competitive against alternative platforms.</p>
<h3>New Authors Considering Draft2Digital</h3>
<p>You face the full cost: $20 to create your account, plus the risk of annual fees if your books do not reach $100/year in sales quickly. Many new authors take 6-18 months to reach that threshold, meaning you could pay $40-60 in fees before you earn enough to waive the maintenance charge.</p>
<hr />

<h2 id="free-alternatives">Best Free Alternatives to Draft2Digital for EPUB Publishing</h2>
<p>If you are looking to distribute EPUB files to major ebook stores without paying activation or maintenance fees, here are the strongest alternatives in 2026:</p>

<h3>1. Kobo Writing Life — The Top EPUB-Native Platform</h3>
<p><strong>Kobo Writing Life</strong> is free to join, charges no activation or annual fees, and takes only the standard 30% retailer commission (you keep 70% on books priced $0.99-$12.99). It is the most popular direct alternative because:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Native EPUB support.</strong> Upload your EPUB, and Kobo converts it to its KEPUB format automatically. No MOBI-to-EPUB conversion needed.</li>
<li><strong>190-country reach.</strong> Kobo sells in Canada, the UK, France, Japan, Australia, and dozens more. It also distributes to public libraries via OverDrive.</li>
<li><strong>No exclusivity required.</strong> Publish on Kobo and everywhere else simultaneously.</li>
<li><strong>Promotional tools.</strong> Kobo allows limited-time price promotions and countdown deals at no extra cost.</li>
</ul>
<p>The trade-off: Kobo's audience is smaller than Amazon's, but among EPUB readers migrating away from the Kindle ecosystem, Kobo is the fastest-growing platform in 2026.</p>

<h3>2. StreetLib — The Free Aggregator with Broad Reach</h3>
<p><strong>StreetLib</strong> (formerly StreetLib Write) is a free ebook distribution platform that competes directly with Draft2Digital. It offers free basic distribution to 10+ stores including Kobo, Apple Books, Google Play Books, Barnes &amp; Noble (Nook), and Tolino. Key features:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Free tier available.</strong> Basic distribution costs nothing. StreetLib takes a commission only on sales (18% commission on ebook sales).</li>
<li><strong>EPUB-native workflow.</strong> Upload EPUB or DOCX; StreetLib validates the file and auto-converts for partner stores.</li>
<li><strong>No maintenance fees.</strong> Unlike Draft2Digital's new model, StreetLib does not charge annual fees for low-earning accounts.</li>
<li><strong>Print-on-demand.</strong> StreetLib also handles print distribution through IngramSpark.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. PublishDrive — Global Distribution with Zero Upfront Fees</h3>
<p><strong>PublishDrive</strong> is another Draft2Digital competitor that charges no setup or annual fees. It distributes EPUB files to 400+ stores and 240+ countries, including Kobo, Apple Books, Google Play, Barnes &amp; Noble, Tolino, and over 250 library systems worldwide.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Zero upfront fees</strong> for the standard plan.</li>
<li><strong>Choose your commission.</strong> PublishDrive offers a sliding commission model — you can select a higher commission rate for more services or a lower rate for basic distribution.</li>
<li><strong>Real-time analytics.</strong> Dashboard shows sales, royalties, and reader demographics across all stores.</li>
<li><strong>Audiobook distribution.</strong> Also distributes audiobooks to Audible, Apple Books, and Google Play.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Apple Books Direct Publishing — Premium Reach, No Fees</h3>
<p>If you want to reach the premium ebook audience, <strong>Apple Books</strong> accepts EPUB directly through its Books Partner portal. There are zero setup fees, zero annual fees, and you keep 70% of revenue on books priced above $1.99.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Beautiful EPUB rendering.</strong> Apple Books on iPad and Mac renders EPUB with custom fonts, embedded video, and advanced CSS — features most competitors strip out.</li>
<li><strong>Free ISBN.</strong> Apple provides a free ISBN for your ebook in most territories.</li>
<li><strong>Editorial curation.</strong> Apple Books curates its storefront, giving quality-focused titles visibility they might not get on algorithm-driven stores.</li>
</ul>
<p>The downside: you must manage your account individually rather than distributing to 10+ stores from one dashboard.</p>

<h3>5. Direct Sales via Gumroad or Payhip</h3>
<p>Selling EPUB directly to readers is the ultimate way to avoid platform fees entirely. <strong>Gumroad</strong> and <strong>Payhip</strong> let you sell EPUB files directly, keep 90-97% after payment processing (typically 2.9% + $0.30 per sale), and maintain full control over pricing and customer relationships.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gumroad:</strong> Best for authors who already have a following. Built-in email lists, discount codes, and affiliate tools.</li>
<li><strong>Payhip:</strong> Better EU VAT handling, PDF stamping for watermarking, and subscription options for serialized content.</li>
</ul>
<p>Direct sales mean no DRM, no distributor commissions, and no annual fees. The trade-off is entirely manual marketing and customer support.</p>
